      <Docs>
        <summary>
        </summary>
        <value>the actions of this Permission.</value>
        <remarks>
          <para>Returns the actions as a String. This is abstract so subclasses can defer creating a String representation until one is needed. Subclasses should always return actions in what they consider to be their canonical form. For example, two FilePermission objects created via the following:</para>
          <code lang="text/java">   perm1 = new FilePermission(p1,"read,write");
   perm2 = new FilePermission(p2,"write,read");</code>
          <para>both return "read,write" when the getActions method is invoked.</para>
          <para><format type="text/html"><a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/security/Permission.html#getActions()" title="Reference documentation">Java reference for <code>java.security.Permission.getActions</code>.</a></format></para>
